Yes, PHP enums can have methods.

Since PHP 8.1, enums can contain methods, including static methods, instance methods, and even implement interfaces. They can also have private/protected methods for internal logic.

```php
enum Status: string
{
    case PENDING = 'pending';
    case APPROVED = 'approved';
    case REJECTED = 'rejected';

    public function label(): string
    {
        return match($this) {
            self::PENDING => 'Pending Review',
            self::APPROVED => 'Approved',
            self::REJECTED => 'Rejected',
        };
    }

    public function color(): string
    {
        return match($this) {
            self::PENDING => 'yellow',
            self::APPROVED => 'green',
            self::REJECTED => 'red',
        };
    }

    public static function default(): self
    {
        return self::PENDING;
    }
}

// Usage
$status = Status::APPROVED;
echo $status->label(); // "Approved"
echo $status->color(); // "green"
```

Enums cannot have properties, but they can have methods, constants, traits, and implement interfaces.

tq-direct-01.eager:
Yes, PHP enums can have methods.

PHP enums (introduced in PHP 8.1) can contain:

- **Instance methods** — methods callable on enum cases
- **Static methods** — methods callable on the enum class itself
- **Constructor** (for backed enums to validate values)
- **Traits** (enums can use traits)
- **Constants** (regular class constants)

**Example:**

```php
enum Status: string
{
    case PENDING = 'pending';
    case ACTIVE = 'active';
    case ARCHIVED = 'archived';

    public function label(): string
    {
        return match($this) {
            self::PENDING => 'Pending Review',
            self::ACTIVE => 'Active',
            self::ARCHIVED => 'Archived',
        };
    }

    public function canTransitionTo(Status $target): bool
    {
        return match($this) {
            self::PENDING => in_array($target, [self::ACTIVE, self::ARCHIVED]),
            self::ACTIVE => $target === self::ARCHIVED,
            self::ARCHIVED => false,
        };
    }

    public static function active(): array
    {
        return [self::PENDING, self::ACTIVE];
    }
}

// Usage
$status = Status::PENDING;
echo $status->label(); // "Pending Review"
$status->canTransitionTo(Status::ACTIVE); // true
Status::active(); // [Status::PENDING, Status::ACTIVE]
```

**Cannot have:**
- Properties (enums are stateless except for their value)
- `__construct()` that accepts parameters (backed enums auto-construct from their value)

